  "readTime": 1,
  "content": "<table align=\"center\" border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\">\n<div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr>\n<td align=\"left\" style=\"padding-left: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p>#​674 — November 19, 2026</p></td>\n<td align=\"right\" style=\"padding-right: 4px; font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"><p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193142/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Web Version</a></p></td>\n</tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0 12px;\"><p>Postgres Weekly</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\"><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.0em;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193097/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; line-height: 1.0em;\" target=\"_blank\"><img loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/w_1280,e_sharpen:60,q_auto/wvk9yfa9brpepedo3stv.jpg\" style=\"line-height: 1.0em;\" width=\"640\"/></a>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>Postgres 19 Released</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>It might be GTA VI's release day, but we all know the day's <em>true</em> big release… <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193097/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Postgres 19 is here</a>! It's headlined by online table repacking and new ways to keep query plans stable. Some highlights:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193098/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>REPACK</code></a> reclaims bloat and can reorder a table to match an index, and <code>REPACK (CONCURRENTLY)</code> does it without blocking reads or writes until the final swap. It supersedes <code>VACUUM FULL</code> and <code>CLUSTER</code> (which remain for compatibility).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>For developers, <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193099/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO SELECT</code></a> handles 'get or create' in one round trip, <code>COPY</code> can output JSON, and window functions gain <code>IGNORE NULLS</code>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193100/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>pg_plan_advice</code></a> extension lets you experiment with and guide plan choices, and <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193101/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>pg_stash_advice</code></a> applies advice automatically by query ID, so you can fix a slow plan without changing your app.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Logical replication can be turned on without a restart, <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193102/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">sequences can now be replicated</a>, and <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193103/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\"><code>WAIT FOR LSN</code></a> gives read-your-writes consistency on standbys.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>And, of course, <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193104/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">much, much more</a>, all covered in the release notes.</p>\n<p>PostgreSQL Global Development Group</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<div style=\"margin-top: 14px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"></div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193096/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\"><img height=\"115\" loading=\"lazy\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" src=\"https://res.cloudinary.com/cpress/image/upload/c_limit,w_480,h_480,q_auto/copm/48f775ba.png\" style=\"padding-top: 12px; padding-left: 12px; line-height: 100%;\" width=\"115\"/></a>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193096/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.1em; line-height: 1.4em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.tigerdata.com\">Your Partition Maintenance Shouldn’t Be a Cron Job</a></span> — Native partitioning means creating and attaching partitions on a schedule. Hypertables create chunks automatically as data arrives, and compression cuts storage by up to 95% on older ones. Same Postgres. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193096/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Start on Tiger Cloud with a $1000 credit</a>.</p>\n<p>Tiger Data (creators of TimescaleDB) <span style=\"margin-left: 4px; font-size: 0.9em; color: #997 !important; padding: 1px 4px;\">sponsor</span></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<div style=\"margin-top: 14px; margin-bottom: 8px;\"></div>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>BEFORE YOU UPGRADE TO POSTGRES 19:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Indexes using full text search, <code>pg_trgm</code> or <code>fuzzystrmatch</code> <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193105/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">may need a reindex after <code>pg_upgrade</code></a>. Jeff Davis explains why there's no upgrade check for it.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><code>pg_upgrade --check</code> <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193106/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">can pass and the upgrade still fail</a> if a role or database stores <code>standard_conforming_strings = off</code>, or you've added your own <code>uuid</code> to <code>bytea</code> cast (now built in).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>After a <code>pg_restore</code>, <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193107/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">run <code>VACUUM ANALYZE</code></a>. Nikolay saw a query take 32.6s instead of 23ms without it, and the first read after a restore can be costly (on 19, particularly).</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193108/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">JIT is now off by default</a>, as its cost estimates proved unreliable. If you run a lot of large analytical queries, you may want to turn it back on. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193109/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">When to JIT?</a> has more detail on this.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><span>⭐</span> <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193110/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"www.postgresql.org\">Postgres 18.7, 17.12, 16.16, 15.20, and 14.25 Released</a></span> — The latest minor releases fix 52 security issues and Postgres 14 goes 'end of life'. <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193111/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Lukas Fittl explains who's exposed</a> (often any logged-in user) and Devrim Gündüz <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193112/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">shows why extensions must be updated with the server</a>. See <em>\"Updating\"</em> for reindexing steps.</p>\n<p>PostgreSQL Global Development Group </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>IN BRIEF:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>The AI-generated bug reports are still flooding in. Christophe Pettus asks people to <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193113/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">stop sending unchecked ones</a>, and depesz <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193114/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">charts the effect on the lists</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🎂 Happy 20th birthday to <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193115/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">osm2pgsql</a>, the tool that loads OpenStreetMap data into Postgres and powers many major OSM maps.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>🇺🇸 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193116/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">PG DATA 2027</a> takes place June 11–12 in Chicago. The <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193117/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">CFP is open</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Microsoft's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193118/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Azure HorizonDB is now generally available</a>.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193119/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"malisper.me\">Fixing Postgres's Biggest Mistakes</a></span> — Michael follows up his <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193120/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">'four horsemen' of Postgres outages</a> by showing how <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193121/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">pgrust</a>, his team's Rust reimplementation, is tackling certain Postgres shortcomings, from storage that needs no <code>VACUUM</code> to a planner that learns from its mistakes.</p>\n<p>Michael Malis </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193122/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"mehmetince.net\">How a 22-Year-Old Type Confusion Became RCE</a></span> — Exploiting an extension on a managed Postgres service led Mehmet into core, where changing a composite type's field and reading the old value back became a reliable way to run code. It's one of many reports behind <code>CVE-2026-6460</code>, fixed in the latest releases (above).</p>\n<p>Mehmet Ince </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193123/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"smokentar.com\">Benchmarking DigitalOcean's New 'Advanced' Managed Postgres</a></span> — DigitalOcean's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193124/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">launch post</a> teased lower latency and higher throughput, but after 59 hours of benchmarks across 100+ databases, Dimitar found the opposite in his tests.</p>\n<p>Dimitar Atanasov </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193125/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Large Postgres Instances Love Huge Pages</a> – If your <code>shared_buffers</code> runs to tens of GBs, leaving huge pages unconfigured can cost you 10-20% throughput… <cite>Kaarel Moppel</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193126/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Ten Ways a <code>glibc</code> Change Can Break Your Postgres Cluster</a> – B-tree indexes are just the start, with a way to detect each failure. <cite>Pablo Svampa (Percona)</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193127/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">How One Pooler Setting Cut a Postgres Server's Memory Use</a> – A quick PgBouncer tip. <cite>Dinesh Kumar</cite></p>\n<p>📄 <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193128/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">How a Broken Release Name Overloaded Sentry's Postgres Database</a> <cite>Michael Warkentin (Sentry)</cite></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px;\">\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>📰 Classifieds</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p>🧾 Invoices arriving as PDFs? <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193129/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Foxit’s API</a> pulls line items, totals, dates and vendors into JSON, ready to load straight into Postgres.</p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><strong>RELEASES AND CODE:</strong></p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193130/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"github.com\">pgrx 0.20: Build Postgres Extensions in Rust</a></span> — <em>\"A big one\"</em> for the framework for building extensions with Rust, which now generates bindings for Postgres's C macros and inline functions automatically, and is Postgres 19 ready.</p>\n<p>PgCentral Foundation </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\"><p>💡 pgrx is now a pretty popular way to build extensions, with ParadeDB's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193131/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_search</a>, Supabase's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193132/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">pg_graphql</a>, Tiger Data's <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193133/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">pgvectorscale</a> and <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193134/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 600;\" target=\"_blank\">PostgresML</a> all built on it.</p></td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 600; font-size: 1.1em; color: #000;\"><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193135/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-size: 1.05em;\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"gnomon.bitsinfra.com\">Gnomon: Git-Style Branching/Merging for Postgres Data</a></span> — An extension that brings branches, diffs, merges and undo to data in Postgres, with branches accessible behind different connection strings. New and experimental, and reminds me of the established Postgres-compatible (but <em>not</em> Postgres) <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193136/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb;\" target=\"_blank\">Doltgres</a>.</p>\n<p>bitsinfra </p>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em; padding: 0px 15px;\">\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193137/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">PgDog 0.1.65</a> – Rust-powered pooler and sharding proxy. Adds cross-shard CTEs and <code>HAVING</code>, and fixes wrong results from some cross-shard <code>GROUP BY</code> queries.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193138/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Atlas 1.4</a> – The schema-as-code tool adds <code>atlas security scan</code> to report CVEs affecting your database servers and extensions.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193139/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">Postbird 0.9</a> – A long-dormant open source Postgres GUI returns, rewritten on <a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193140/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important;\" target=\"_blank\">Electrobun</a>.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><a href=\"https://postgresweekly.com/link/193141/rss\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" style=\"color: #0088bb; font-weight: 500 !important; font-size: 1.08em; line-height: 1.5em;\" target=\"_blank\">pgAudit 19.0</a> – The audit logging extension is ready for Postgres 19.</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</td></tr></table>\n<table border=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><td style=\"font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.48em;\"></td></tr></table>\n</div>\n</td></tr>\n</table>\n",
  "attachments": []
}